>This blog used to be about things like leadership and system patterns. I g=
uess AI has taken it over, and I=E2=80=99m a bit sorry about that. I use th=
is writing to make myself more present in the moment during the week - to o=
bserve patterns and think about things I might write about - and most of wh=
at I have been doing for the last little bit is thinking about AI, and then=
 more recently, doing a lot of coding with the tools we=E2=80=99ve built. S=
o, it=E2=80=99s all AI now, at least for a bit. </p><p>But I actually don=
=E2=80=99t think that=E2=80=99s bad, even if you aren=E2=80=99t a programme=
r and don=E2=80=99t care. The coding world is showing how the AI world will=
 likely unfold for other uses and professions. =E2=80=9CCode goes first=E2=
=80=9D because it=E2=80=99s easier for the models to deal with and the prog=
ramming community (well, some of it, anyway) are already primed to understa=
nd and use it. </p><p>When I sat down to write this, I had a bit more troub=
le than usual - there are a lot of thoughts in my head right now! So I am g=
oing to do something a bit different. Usually I just have, and write, one t=
hought a week. But I think there are a bunch of things that fit together us=
efully, so I am going to indulge in a longer post this time. Let=E2=80=99s =
start with a good old =E2=80=9Cinnovation principle=E2=80=9D that I used to=
 write about a lot. </p><h5>Why Not/What If</h5><p>Something I learned long=
 ago when doing Writely (which became Google Docs), is that there is someth=
ing really strange that happens when a truly disruptive idea emerges. These=
 ideas are really obvious in retrospect, so at the time people must be real=
ly happy to see them, right? </p><p>Actually, no. What really happens is th=
at people start by feeling an emotion - there is an uncomfortable feeling w=
hen your world view is challenged. There are essentially two ways to deal w=
ith this emotion - you can choose to think that your world view is right, a=
nd the new idea is wrong, or you can choose to update your world view to ac=
commodate the new idea.</p><p>These two states manifest in either telling =
=E2=80=9Cwhy not=E2=80=9D stories - inventing reasons why the new thing is =
wrong, or =E2=80=9Cwhat if=E2=80=9D stories - what happens if it is. Ordina=
ry changes don=E2=80=99t get this behavior, you get a much broader spread o=
f reactions. But big category changes get =E2=80=9CI love it=E2=80=9D/ =E2=
=80=9CI hate it=E2=80=9D and not much inbetween. </p><p>We are seeing this =
in the agentic coding world. There are still lots of people who strongly be=
lieve it won=E2=80=99t work, it=E2=80=99s impossible to build code that way=
, and so on. And then..I spend time in communities of people who are very s=
uccessfully using these tools, all day long (me included). It=E2=80=99s lik=
e in the early days of Writely, when we had about a half million happy user=
s, and people would still tell me that the browser could never be used to b=
uild a satisfying app. </p><p>If you are skeptical, the only thing I can sa=
y is: try to examine that skepticism as honestly as you can, and talk to pe=
ople who aren=E2=80=99t with an open mind, if you can find them. </p><h5>In=
ternet Stages</h5><p>The other thing that I think is going on with AI broad=
ly and also with coding has to do with timing. People think the internet ha=
ppened all at once, but it didn=E2=80=99t - the browser was 1993, the dotco=
m crash was 2000, Writely was 2005 and the iPhone wasn=E2=80=99t even until=
 2006! We had a lot of tools, infrastructure, best practices and work to do=
 to make the internet what it is today. </p><p>The last few years of AI hav=
e been amazing but there have been problems. We=E2=80=99ve had the idea of =
agents, but they haven=E2=80=99t really worked as well as we want. Models a=
re good but not great, and still too inaccurate for meaningful work. </p><p=
>It=E2=80=99s taken a while to fix enough of those issues, and to understan=
d how to make use of the increased capabilities. But now, we have agentic c=
oding frameworks that are astoundingly good - it feels like AI is coming in=
to focus, the way the internet did after the first few rough years. This is=
 a fairly common pattern with new tech - we get the idea before we get the =
robust implementation. AI coding is showing that maturation process now, or=
 at least a next step in it. </p><h5>Attention Saturation</h5><p>This is an=
other interesting thing that=E2=80=99s starting to emerge. Coding agents ar=
e true agents - you can easily give them an instruction, and they will do a=
 fairly large amount of work for you. So, it=E2=80=99s very easy (if you ha=
ve the budget) to start a whole bunch of new coding tasks - if you have an =
idea, it=E2=80=99s just a minute or so to start up a new agent. </p><p>But =
you still have to pay attention to the output. So you wind up saturating yo=
urself - you will have as many things going on as you can pay attention to.=
 Which means your co-workers will, too. This makes it hard to collaborate -=
 everyone is busy all the time. I wrote about this last week, and I can say=
 - it=E2=80=99s only getting worse! It=E2=80=99s very much the case that, a=
s I get better at using the tools, and build new tools to build my own leve=
rage, I just do more work, not less! </p><p>Maybe that=E2=80=99s just how h=
umans are. We have to do much less work to feed ourselves than we did 200 y=
ears ago. We don=E2=80=99t spend that efficiency on relaxing; we spend it o=
n ever richer and more complex lives. Most of us live lives that would have=
 been the envy of our ancestors. I=E2=80=99ve suspected this will be the ca=
se with AI - it won=E2=80=99t replace jobs, it will change them, and we mig=
ht well wind up being more busy, not less. So far, that=E2=80=99s very much=
 my personal experience. </p><h5>The hard part is picking the work</h5><p><=
span>Finally, this goes along with the attention issue above. Because it=E2=
=80=99s so easy to start things now with these tools, you have to have good=
 taste in what you start. </span><strong>It=E2=80=99s not hard to do work n=
ow, it=E2=80=99s hard to pick what work to do.</strong><span> I suspect thi=
s is a deep truth of the AI age - as agents get more powerful and cheaper, =
the actual mechanics of the work will matter less. What will matter is the =
taste and judgement of deciding what to do. </span></p><p>We could take cal=
ories as an analogy here. As food got cheaper, safer, more accessible and c=
heaper, the key skill isn=E2=80=99t being able to find food (almost everyon=
e can do that), it=E2=80=99s deciding what and how much to eat to stay heal=
thy. As thinking gets cheaper, safer, and more accessible, perhaps the same=
 will be true. </p><p></p><p><span>All of this was about coding but we are =
using these tools for other things. I built a whole website talking about o=
ur tool, using it, in just a few minutes this week: </span><a href=3D"https=
://ramparte.github.io/amplifier-stories/">Amplifier Stories</a><span>. This=
 is the only way I do them now. We build research papers with it. It=E2=80=
=99s usually the first place I go to do any task, and often, if it can=E2=
=80=99t do what I want, I can build a tool for that</span><span style=3D"mi=
n-width: 0px;" data-state=3D"closed"><a data-component-name=3D"FootnoteAnch=
orToDOM" id=3D"footnote-anchor-1" href=3D"https://sundaylettersfromsam.subs=
tack.com/p/the-hard-part-isnt-doing-the-work#footnote-1" target=3D"_self" c=
lass=3D"footnote-anchor">1</a></span><span>. </span></p><p>Code goes first.=
 Don=E2=80=99t be lulled into thinking =E2=80=9Cthat=E2=80=99s all there is=
=E2=80=9D with AI. It=E2=80=99s still moving. The coding communities are ab=
solutely on fire at the moment. </p><p data-attrs=3D"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot=
